rocessed  and I was assigned to processing one day  and as a routine part of the questioning  we have to ask  they have scars or tattoos and the guy  said he had scars and when he lifted up  his shirt to show me the scars there  were lash marks all over his back when I  asked him what those were from he  explained that he refused to carry drugs  for a smuggler and they tied him up and  whipped him for it I didn't feel good  about sending that guy back and that was  a dramatic example but there's countless  others that didn't have a dramatic story  like that that they just want a better  life and I stopped being able to  reconcile that what also changed his  mind was listening to podcasts while  driving around the border 8 to 10 hours  a day specifically a show called  unregistered hosted by historian and  recent contributor Thaddeus Russell one  particular episode featuring author and  sex worker  Maggie MacNeil made him reexamine his  fundamental beliefs and I've actually  had people go oh yeah a [ __ ] - I'll go  no no no I mean an actual prostitute  ring is gonna actually take money for  sex and then they'll be like oh oh oh  because they're thinking I mean [ __ ]  right how did the idea is being  expressed there changed the way you  started to think about your job so there  were two guests of feds that when I  first saw them I wasn't completely  thrilled I didn't really want to hear  what they had to say I thought and one  of those is Maggie MacNeil and Connor  Habib who's a gay porn star I don't have  anything morally against either those  lifestyles but I also didn't really want  to hear about it but once I finally did  download and listen to those episodes I  was blown away I mean they had so much  more to offer  and so much more to say than these  little categories that we put them in as  a pornstar or prostitute they're  fascinating people they're brave they're  smart they're heroic  this small bias in my mind almost  stopped me from listening to these  people's stories and how much enrichment  I got from listening to their stories so  then it made me start to take stock of  the rest of my life if I had these  biases that I didn't really realize I  had what else is there how do I really  feel about the drug war how do I really  feel about prisons and immigration  naturally came up as one of those  opinions and so what are your views  about immigration now I think we're  moving in the wrong direction I think  we're moving towards more restriction  more enforcement and I think there  should be less because Bryan Caplan the  economist says if there's not a good  reason to keep you out come on in he  basically says that if there's no  communicable disease no violent past  there's no reason that you shouldn't be  allowed to come in and try to make a  life for yourself I want to see a freer  world I want people to take charge of  their lives I want people to be able to  make their own choices and I don't want  an imaginary boundary to get in the way  of that they're being spoken about as if  they are pieces to be moved around or  assets to be utilized like know their  people deport all criminal aliens and  dismantle every last criminal gang in  cartel threatening our citizens we're  told from the Trump administration that  the reason that we need to get tough on  the border is to stop you know ms-13  members from coming over criminal  violent criminals and drug traffickers  and child traffickers those people do  exist and we we do come across them but  I no longer believe that immigration  policy or immigration restriction is the  solution  that problem if you have problems with  drug trafficking and violent gangs which  are also linked to do drugs most of the  time  well let's legalize drugs in the United  States and that takes a significant  amount of power out of those guy's hands  we can't fix the problem just by more  restriction Childress recently appeared  on Russell's podcast for a long-form  discussion about his intellectual  journey and ultimate decision to leave  the Border Patrol  he's now working on a maintenance crew  at an apartment complex alongside  several Mexican immigrants if what I  have to say has any effect I hope that  it is that people will take inventory of  their life and take inventory of their  views think about why you believe what  you believe and if you can go through it  and think yeah these beliefs are firmly  rooted good that's gonna help you sleep  at night that's gonna help you lead a  peaceful life but if it's not I think  that's valuable information to have  [Music]
 

